  <h1>India’s Unemployment Challenge — Beyond Jobless Growth</h1>
  <div><em>Indian Express | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>Despite being one of the world’s fastest-growing economies, India continues to grapple with high unemployment and underemployment. The Indian Express in 2024 highlighted the structural nature of India’s jobs crisis, describing it as a challenge that goes “beyond jobless growth.” This is crucial for UPSC as it touches upon economy, governance, and social justice.
________________________________________
Current Employment Scenario
•	Unemployment Rate: CMIE data (2024) ~7–8%, with urban unemployment higher than rural.
•	Youth Unemployment: ~23% for ages 20–24, reflecting mismatch between education and skills.
•	Sectoral Shifts:
o	Agriculture still employs ~45% but contributes only 18% to GDP.
o	Manufacturing stagnated at ~16–17% of GDP (far below China’s ~27%).
o	Services dominate but with limited capacity for mass employment.
________________________________________
Drivers of the Jobs Crisis
1.	Structural Issues
o	Insufficient manufacturing base (“Make in India” progress limited).
o	Low productivity in agriculture.
2.	Education–Skill Mismatch
o	Higher education expansion without adequate skill development.
o	Employers report shortage of job-ready graduates.
3.	Informality
o	~80–85% of workforce in informal jobs, lacking security and social protection.
4.	Technology Disruption
o	Automation and AI reducing low-skill job opportunities.
5.	Policy Gaps
o	Labour law reforms unevenly implemented across states.
o	Limited focus on labour-intensive industries (textiles, toys, footwear).
________________________________________
Government Responses
•	PM Kaushal Vikas Yojana (PMKVY): Training millions in vocational skills.
•	Production-Linked Incentives (PLI): Attracting investment in electronics, EVs, textiles.
•	Startup India & Digital India: Encouraging entrepreneurship and gig economy.
•	Mahatma Gandhi NREGA: Rural employment safety net.
________________________________________
Challenges
•	Skill Gaps: Training quality inconsistent.
•	Gender Inequality: Female labour force participation still ~25%, among the lowest globally.
•	Regional Disparities: Job creation concentrated in certain states.
•	Quality vs Quantity: New jobs often low-paying gig or informal work.
________________________________________
UPSC Relevance
•	GS-III (Economy): Growth vs employment; informal sector reforms.
•	GS-II (Governance): Welfare schemes for workers, labour reforms.
•	Essay:
o	“India’s demographic dividend: a window of opportunity or a ticking time bomb?”
o	“Jobless growth undermines the foundations of inclusive development.”
•	PSIR Paper II: Political economy of development; employment as legitimacy of governance.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Boost Manufacturing: Focus on labour-intensive sectors; leverage PLI for MSMEs.<br/>2.	Education Reforms: Align curriculum with market needs; vocational courses from school level.<br/>3.	Formalization: Incentivize small firms to register, adopt digital systems.<br/>4.	Gender Inclusion: Invest in childcare, safety, and flexible jobs to raise women’s participation.<br/>5.	Green & Digital Jobs: Promote renewable energy, EVs, and AI-enabled services.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>India’s unemployment challenge is not just about numbers but about the quality and inclusivity of jobs. Without addressing structural bottlenecks in manufacturing, skills, and informality, the demographic dividend may turn into a liability. For UPSC aspirants, the issue exemplifies the governance challenge of translating growth into employment.</p>
